{"item_id":"E-WRITE-314","title":"Spot survivorship bias before it becomes a bad sample","body":"If you run a small business, survivorship bias usually shows up as a simple decision mistake: you only see the cases that made it through. You hear from existing customers, successful orders, or clean supplier records, then assume the missing cases would have looked the same. They often do not. That is not a theory problem; it is a workflow problem.

The bounded question is: what decision are you making from samples right now, and how would you know early if that decision was going to fail? That can be product fit, customer segment choice, supplier quality, or a process rule that looks fine until the edge cases arrive. If the sample is incomplete, AI is not the answer by itself. If the underlying cases can be logged or exported, a simple check may help surface gaps. If the cases are scattered, anecdotal, or depend on judgment, the better move may be to keep it manual for now. Unhelpful, but honest.

A practical next step is to write down one recent decision made from incomplete data. List what you had, what was missing, and what would have changed if the missing cases had been visible at the start. Once that is clear, you can decide whether the right investment is a manual rule, a spreadsheet check, or a bounded AI-assisted workflow. No guesswork, no magic, just a cleaner sample."}

